# VeloPhi supplement to the PHREEQC databases (26.64, D262): species of school chemistry that phreeqc.dat, wateq4f.dat
# and minteq.v4.dat do not carry. PHREEQC syntax, read by scripts/chem/build_aqueous.py after the three files.
#
# Every entry names its source. "hand" means the value was typed from the cited reference and is on the list of
# hand-entered values in docs/chem/DATA_SOURCES.md (to be checked a second time against the reference before a
# value is relied on outside teaching). Oxidation states that are components of their own are bracket elements:
# [S4] sulfite sulfur, [S2] thiosulfate sulfur (average state), [Cl1] hypochlorite chlorine, [O-1] peroxide oxygen …
# A group that never comes apart in the bench (oxalate, thiocyanate, the hexacyanoferrates) is an element of its
# own, as the organic ligands are in minteq.v4.dat.
#
# References
#   NIST46  Martell, Smith, Motekaitis: NIST Critically Selected Stability Constants of Metal Complexes, Standard
#           Reference Database 46, version 8.0 (2004). Values at 25 °C and zero ionic strength.
#   SANDER  R. Sander: Compilation of Henry's law constants (version 4.0) for water as solvent, Atmos. Chem. Phys. 15,
#           4399–4981 (2015), CC BY 3.0. H^cp converted to mol/(kg·atm); d ln H / d(1/T) converted to ΔH.
#   CRC     CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics, 97th ed. (2016): dissociation constants of inorganic acids;
#           solubility product constants.
#   WANG    T. X. Wang, D. W. Margerum: Kinetics of reversible chlorine hydrolysis, Inorg. Chem. 33, 1050 (1994).

SOLUTION_MASTER_SPECIES
[S4]		[S4]O3-2	0	80.06	80.06
[S2]		[S2]2O3-2	0	112.13	112.13
[Cl1]		[Cl1]O-		0	51.45	51.45
[Cl5]		[Cl5]O3-	0	83.45	83.45
[Cl7]		[Cl7]O4-	0	99.45	99.45
[O-1]		H2[O-1]2	0	34.014	34.014
[I0]		[I0]2		0	253.8	253.8
[Br0]		[Br0]2		0	159.81	159.81
[N2]		[N2]O		0	30.006	30.006
[N4]		[N4]O2		0	46.005	46.005
[C2]		[C2]O		0	28.010	28.010
[C-4]		[C-4]H4		0	16.043	16.043
[I5]		[I5]O3-		0	174.903	174.903
[Br5]		[Br5]O3-	0	127.901	127.901
[S25]		[S25]4O6-2	0	224.23	224.23
[S7]		[S7]2O8-2	0	192.11	192.11
Oxalate		Oxalate-2	0	88.02	88.02
Thiocyanate	Thiocyanate-	0	58.08	58.08
Ferricyanide	Ferricyanide-3	0	211.95	211.95
Ferrocyanide	Ferrocyanide-4	0	211.95	211.95

SOLUTION_SPECIES
[S4]O3-2 = [S4]O3-2
[S2]2O3-2 = [S2]2O3-2
[Cl1]O- = [Cl1]O-
[Cl5]O3- = [Cl5]O3-
[Cl7]O4- = [Cl7]O4-
H2[O-1]2 = H2[O-1]2
[I0]2 = [I0]2
[Br0]2 = [Br0]2
[N2]O = [N2]O
[N4]O2 = [N4]O2
[C2]O = [C2]O
[C-4]H4 = [C-4]H4
[I5]O3- = [I5]O3-
[Br5]O3- = [Br5]O3-
[S25]4O6-2 = [S25]4O6-2
[S7]2O8-2 = [S7]2O8-2
Oxalate-2 = Oxalate-2
Thiocyanate- = Thiocyanate-
Ferricyanide-3 = Ferricyanide-3
Ferrocyanide-4 = Ferrocyanide-4

# Sulfurous acid: pKa1 1.86, pKa2 7.20 (NIST46, hand); enthalpies of protonation from the same source
[S4]O3-2 + H+ = H[S4]O3-
	log_k	7.20
	delta_h	3.6 kJ
[S4]O3-2 + 2 H+ = [S4]O2 + H2O
	log_k	9.06
	delta_h	21.4 kJ

# Thiosulfuric acid: pKa2 1.6 (NIST46, hand)
[S2]2O3-2 + H+ = H[S2]2O3-
	log_k	1.6

# Hypochlorous acid: pKa 7.53 (NIST46, hand; ΔH of protonation −13.8 kJ/mol)
[Cl1]O- + H+ = H[Cl1]O
	log_k	7.53
	delta_h	-13.8 kJ
# Dissolved chlorine: Cl2 + H2O = HClO + H+ + Cl-, K = 5.1e-4 at 25 °C and zero ionic strength (WANG, hand)
[Cl1]O- + 2 H+ + Cl- = Cl[Cl1] + H2O
	log_k	10.82
# Trichloride is left out (K ≈ 0.18).

# Hydrogen peroxide: pKa 11.62 (CRC, hand)
H2[O-1]2 = H[O-1]2- + H+
	log_k	-11.62
	delta_h	34.3 kJ

# Triiodide: I2 + I- = I3-, K = 698 at 25 °C (NIST46, hand)
[I0]2 + I- = I[I0]2-
	log_k	2.844
	delta_h	-17.0 kJ

# Iodic acid: pKa 0.78 (CRC, hand). Bromic acid, tetrathionic acid and peroxodisulfuric acid are strong: their anions
# ([Br5] bromate, [S25] tetrathionate with sulfur in the average state +2.5, [S7] peroxodisulfate) carry no further
# species and take part only in the declared redox reactions.
[I5]O3- + H+ = H[I5]O3
	log_k	0.78

# Oxalic acid: pKa1 1.252, pKa2 4.266 (NIST46, hand)
Oxalate-2 + H+ = H(Oxalate)-
	log_k	4.266
	delta_h	6.8 kJ
Oxalate-2 + 2 H+ = H2(Oxalate)
	log_k	5.518
	delta_h	10.6 kJ
Ca+2 + Oxalate-2 = Ca(Oxalate)
	log_k	3.19
Mg+2 + Oxalate-2 = Mg(Oxalate)
	log_k	3.62
Fe+3 + Oxalate-2 = Fe(Oxalate)+
	log_k	9.15
Fe+3 + 2 Oxalate-2 = Fe(Oxalate)2-
	log_k	15.45
Fe+3 + 3 Oxalate-2 = Fe(Oxalate)3-3
	log_k	19.83

# Thiocyanate: thiocyanic acid is strong; the iron(III) complexes of the classic equilibrium demonstration (NIST46, hand)
Fe+3 + Thiocyanate- = Fe(Thiocyanate)+2
	log_k	3.02
	delta_h	-6.3 kJ
Fe+3 + 2 Thiocyanate- = Fe(Thiocyanate)2+
	log_k	4.64
Ag+ + 2 Thiocyanate- = Ag(Thiocyanate)2-
	log_k	8.2

# Ammine complexes of copper(II) and zinc (cumulative constants at zero ionic strength; NIST46, hand)
Cu+2 + NH3 = Cu(NH3)+2
	log_k	4.04
	delta_h	-23.4 kJ
Cu+2 + 2 NH3 = Cu(NH3)2+2
	log_k	7.47
	delta_h	-46.4 kJ
Cu+2 + 3 NH3 = Cu(NH3)3+2
	log_k	10.27
	delta_h	-69.5 kJ
Cu+2 + 4 NH3 = Cu(NH3)4+2
	log_k	11.75
	delta_h	-92.0 kJ
Zn+2 + NH3 = Zn(NH3)+2
	log_k	2.21
Zn+2 + 2 NH3 = Zn(NH3)2+2
	log_k	4.50
Zn+2 + 3 NH3 = Zn(NH3)3+2
	log_k	6.86
Zn+2 + 4 NH3 = Zn(NH3)4+2
	log_k	8.89
	delta_h	-61.9 kJ

# Silver thiosulfate complexes (fixing in photography; NIST46, hand)
Ag+ + [S2]2O3-2 = Ag[S2]2O3-
	log_k	8.82
Ag+ + 2 [S2]2O3-2 = Ag([S2]2O3)2-3
	log_k	13.46

PHASES
# Gases: Henry's law constants at 25 °C and their temperature dependence (SANDER, hand)
Cl2(g)
	Cl[Cl1] = Cl[Cl1]
	log_k	-1.032
	delta_h	-19.1 kJ
SO2(g)
	[S4]O2 = [S4]O2
	log_k	0.114
	delta_h	-24.1 kJ
NO(g)
	[N2]O = [N2]O
	log_k	-2.721
	delta_h	-12.5 kJ
NO2(g)
	[N4]O2 = [N4]O2
	log_k	-1.921
	delta_h	-20.8 kJ
CO(g)
	[C2]O = [C2]O
	log_k	-3.009
	delta_h	-10.8 kJ
CH4(g)
	[C-4]H4 = [C-4]H4
	log_k	-2.854
	delta_h	-13.3 kJ
Br2(g)
	[Br0]2 = [Br0]2
	log_k	-0.137
	delta_h	-33.3 kJ
I2(g)
	[I0]2 = [I0]2
	log_k	0.447
	delta_h	-38.2 kJ
# Hydrogen chloride: HCl(g) = H+ + Cl-, K = 2.0e6 mol²/(kg²·atm) (SANDER: Henry constant times acidity constant, hand)
HCl(g)
	HCl = H+ + Cl-
	log_k	6.30
	delta_h	-74.8 kJ
# Nitric acid vapour: HNO3(g) = H+ + NO3-, K = 2.6e6 (SANDER, hand)
HNO3(g)
	HNO3 = H+ + NO3-
	log_k	6.41
	delta_h	-72.3 kJ

# Solids
# Iodine: solubility 1.33e-3 mol/kg at 25 °C (CRC, hand)
I2(s)
	[I0]2 = [I0]2
	log_k	-2.876
	delta_h	22.6 kJ
# Calcium oxalate monohydrate (whewellite): log Ksp −8.69 (NIST46, hand)
Whewellite
	Ca(Oxalate):H2O = Ca+2 + Oxalate-2 + H2O
	log_k	-8.69
# Silver thiocyanate: log Ksp −11.97 (NIST46, hand)
AgSCN
	Ag(Thiocyanate) = Ag+ + Thiocyanate-
	log_k	-11.97
# Sulfites (CRC solubility products, hand)
BaSO3
	Ba[S4]O3 = Ba+2 + [S4]O3-2
	log_k	-9.30
CaSO3:0.5H2O
	Ca[S4]O3:0.5H2O = Ca+2 + [S4]O3-2 + 0.5 H2O
	log_k	-6.51
Ag2SO3
	Ag2[S4]O3 = 2 Ag+ + [S4]O3-2
	log_k	-13.82
# Iodates and silver bromate (CRC solubility products, hand)
AgIO3
	Ag[I5]O3 = Ag+ + [I5]O3-
	log_k	-7.50
Ba(IO3)2:H2O
	Ba([I5]O3)2:H2O = Ba+2 + 2 [I5]O3- + H2O
	log_k	-8.78
Pb(IO3)2
	Pb([I5]O3)2 = Pb+2 + 2 [I5]O3-
	log_k	-12.43
AgBrO3
	Ag[Br5]O3 = Ag+ + [Br5]O3-
	log_k	-4.27
# Prussian-blue type precipitates are outside the model (their composition varies); the two hexacyanoferrate ions
# only colour the solution and take part in the declared redox reactions.
END
